{"question": "A 17-year-old woman presents to your office concerned that she has not had her menstrual period in 4 months. She states that menses began at age of 13 and has been regular until two months ago. She denies sexual activity, and urine pregnancy test is negative. On exam, she appears well-nourished with a BMI of 21 kg/m^2, but you note that she has enlarged cheeks bilaterally and has calluses on the dorsum of her hands. She says that she has been very stressed with school and has recently been binge eating. What other finding do you expect to see in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Malar rash", "B": "Increased blood glucose level", "C": "Erosion of tooth enamel", "D": "Enlarged thyroid", "E": "Elevated estrogen levels"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Gynecology", "Obstetrics", "Endocrinology", "Psychiatry", "Nutrition"], "option_domains": ["Gynecology", "Endocrinology"], "question_analyses": {"Gynecology": "The medical scenario described presents a complex picture of a 17-year-old woman who has missed her menstrual period for four months, despite having regular cycles in the past. The patient's symptoms include the appearance of enlarged cheeks, calluses on the dorsum of her hands, and recent binge eating. These findings are concerning and suggest a potential underlying condition that requires careful evaluation. ### Interpretation of the Condition\n\n1. **Anovulatory Amenorrhea**: The absence of menstruation for four months, despite regular cycles in the past, is indicative of anovulatory amenorrhea. This condition occurs when the ovaries do not release an egg (ovulation) during a menstrual cycle, leading to the cessation of menstruation. 2. **Stress and Binge Eating**: The patient's recent stress and binge eating are significant factors that could be contributing to her amenorrhea. Stress can disrupt the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, leading to hormonal imbalances and amenorrhea. Binge eating can also lead to hormo